{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-9){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -27 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-\\frac{1}{9}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{3}.$ From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{-3}{1}=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-36947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that its augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=9,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-36814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5"] ["math" "-7"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 18{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{9}{10}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{20}(13{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(13(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-37399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{8}(7{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(7(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-36752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{4}(-5{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(-5(3{x}_{3})+7{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-37379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 17 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 17 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-16{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-89419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-83481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-83481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-11{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+11{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{11}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{2} & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{8}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-83481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-83481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-83481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-83481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2(0)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-140719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-36751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-36751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{2}+2(0) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-140717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{20}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2(4{x}_{3})-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-4{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-4}{1}=-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-37403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{7}{1}=7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-140720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5"] ["math" "-7"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-140720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +6)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-6.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-6)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-89427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5{x}_{3}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{8}{4}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-89423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-89423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(-2)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3b55c0911b6d4dd7608b4d6131f7dc7d808085195ecd16d7a39fdfa42b3f2374"} {"question" "q-37385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "q-37385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ea73aa2cacb46f7521a9695b32875383d932db2ba5073d13086d91e44bbafe"} {"question" "e-5490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the matrix $A$ are given below. \nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3,$ then what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/5f1bfa33d135d95e3153e3305da50b69541d88d1cd78b5b6ad030a89861f3e10.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/example-5490.json" "file_sha256" "eee20d717ba6c15dc0d63bfd12ff208af803982fd3e91823bcb4b4c08c16ad1a"} {"question" "e-5490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=x_3.$ From the first equation, we get $x_1=3x_3.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/f2707b01c13749362887e4ae0da130466ae753dccd4bc354600af79f71c3de5f.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/8e7627a6a515086ff53f5ed250a070e08ee2b71d0e4fb3d79e5434218d028624.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{b}{c}=\\dfrac{1}{1}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/example-5490.json" "file_sha256" "eee20d717ba6c15dc0d63bfd12ff208af803982fd3e91823bcb4b4c08c16ad1a"} {"question" "e-5491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/33471f345fdb027b0440014631bf0dadff706ffb76938b67b15db66fd80dcae6.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/example-5491.json" "file_sha256" "1135e7d2d9aafb163b9b54d3dbfde670e7368a0910021d16bfc60b4e1926ad0a"} {"question" "e-5491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_3=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get $x_1=-x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/97b9d021ad0a9800e0b6a2b696c3908f2f5bec64fa496a1c2222ae5b86a7b56b.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/b489148b8e378e6168ddd9dbba18200f10f2788df69aa7100d20b341116102fd.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/example-5491.json" "file_sha256" "1135e7d2d9aafb163b9b54d3dbfde670e7368a0910021d16bfc60b4e1926ad0a"} {"question" "e-5492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/1503285c91d0c320a3720c702b620bfe2156ed713d915b04c22bd35c06a05cd8.png)\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-1$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3=\\big[ a, \\, b, \\, c \\big]^T$ such that the set $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/example-5492.json" "file_sha256" "01dea88ffc5b92790f28f92a5e8754bcea860afd3ebb23148052a268470bfff6"} {"question" "e-5492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is a diagonal matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, $\\lambda_1=1,$ $\\lambda_2=-1$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Thus, the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ must correspond to $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we obtain $x_1=x_2=0.$  Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/7aab21fae627c5fe36b144f4a1e12a2d5e57ef7c2c987fbee64f718c7cbb7404.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/c080641a794b7a65f7893429d32920f39bfb4d87d9193197afd6b48dacc697a3.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{c}=\\dfrac{0}{1}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/example-5492.json" "file_sha256" "01dea88ffc5b92790f28f92a5e8754bcea860afd3ebb23148052a268470bfff6"}] "usage" {"e-5492" [] "e-5491" [] "q-36814" [] "q-37385" [] "e-5490" []} "basis" 2383 "retractions" [[17592186129540 :question/problem "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above.\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 7$, then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}$?"] [17592186129546 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-3$ and $λ_{2} = 2$ of the matrix $A$, given above. Find the third eigenvector $v_{3} = [a, b, c]^{T}$ such that the set ${v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?"] [17592186128875 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the matrix $A$ are given below.\nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3$, then what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186128875 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3$. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{2x_{1} - 6x_{3} = 0; 2x_{1} - 2x_{2} - 4x_{3} = 0; x_{1} - 3x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=x_3$. From the first equation, we get $x_1=3x_3$. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1$, we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{c}=\\dfrac{1}{1}=1\n$$"] [17592186128876 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$."] [17592186128876 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} - (-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{3x_{1} + 3x_{2} + 4x_{3} = 0; 0 = 0; x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}↔R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_3=0$. Substituting this in the first equation, we get $x_1=-x_2$. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186128877 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-1$ of the matrix $A$, given above. Find the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3=\\big[a, \\, b, \\, c \\big]^T$ such that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}$?"] [17592186128877 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a diagonal matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=4\n$$\n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$, where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nThus, the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ must correspond to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=4\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4$. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{-3x_{1} = 0; - 5x_{2} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we obtain $x_1=x_2=0$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1$, we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c}=\\dfrac{0}{1}=0\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127035 17592186127036 17592186127037 17592186127038 17592186127039 17592186127071 17592186127072 17592186127073 17592186127074 17592186127075] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127034 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127034 :answer-field/id] [17592186127034 :answer-field/key] [17592186127034 :answer-field/type] [17592186127070 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127070 :answer-field/id] [17592186127070 :answer-field/key] [17592186127070 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
